{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Korah took</b>. This pericope is well interpreted in Midrash Rabbai Tanḥuma (Korah 2): <b>And Korah took</b> — he took himself to one side, to be separated from the congregation, to stir up controversy over the priesthood. This is what Onkelos translated (Onkelos on Numbers 16:1) ״וְאִתְפְּלֵג״ — he separated himself from the congregation to take hold of a dispute. And so too ״מַה יִּקָּחֲךָ לִבֶּךָ״ (Job 15:12) — what takes you to separate yourself from the rest of mankind; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 16:1). But the view of the Midrash is not like the explanation of the Rav, for they said there: ״וַיִּקַּח״ means nothing other than the language of פְּלִיגָה (separating), his heart took him — in the manner of what is said ״מַה יִּקָּחֲךָ לִבֶּךָ״. And it does not mean that he took himself to one side. And likewise ״מַה יִּקָּחֲךָ לִבֶּךָ״ does not mean that it takes you to one side to separate yourself from the rest of mankind, but rather the intent of the Midrash in ״וַיִּקַּח קֹרַח״ is that he took counsel in his heart to do what would be recounted, for לְקִיחָה (taking) is said of counsel and thought. And likewise ״מַה יִּקָּחֲךָ לִבֶּךָ״ — what thought does your heart take for you, that you think in secret 'there is no judgment and there is no Judge' and you do not reveal it; or ״מַה יִּרְמְזוּן עֵינֶיךָ״ — for through your winks it is evident that you deny divine providence yet you do not state this explicitly, but rather pick a quarrel while concealing yourself. And Eliphaz said this to Job before Job had revealed his thought in clear statement — that the Creator has no providence over the details of lowly created beings — and therefore he said to him (ibid. 22:13) ״וְאָמַרְתָּ מַה יָּדַע אֵל הַבְעַד עֲרָפֶל יִשְׁפֹּט״, and that is the substance of that reply for one who reflects upon it. And likewise the language of לְקִיחָה in the sense of thought: ״קְחוּ מוּסָרִי״ (Proverbs 8:10), ״וּלְבִלְתִּי קַחַת מוּסָר״ (Jeremiah 17:23). They said further in the Midrash (Rabbah 18:16): ״וַיַּחֲלֹק״ ״וַיְדַבֵּר״ ״וַיְצַו קֹרַח״ — Scripture does not say, but rather ״וַיִּקַּח״; what did he take? He took nothing, but rather his heart — Scripture says ״מַה יִּקָּחֲךָ לִבֶּךָ״ — and this is as I have explained. And Onkelos, who translated ״וְאִתְפְּלֵג״, interpreted the meaning and not the wording, according to his practice in many places. And so he translated ״עַל דְּבַר קֹרַח״ (Numbers 17:14) as ״עַל פְּלֻגְתָּא דְּקֹרַח״, and he translated ״בִּדְבַר בִּלְעָם״ (Numbers 31:16) as ״בְּעֵצַת בִּלְעָם״, for he indicates the matter in his translation. And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Numbers 16:1): ״וַיִּקַּח קֹרַח״ — men, as an ellipsis, like ״חֲמוֹר לֶחֶם״ (1 Samuel 16:20). And others said that <b>וְדָתָן</b> is like ״וְאֵלֶּה בְּנֵי צִבְעוֹן וְאַיָּה וַעֲנָה״ (Genesis 36:24), and its proper form would be ״וַיִּקַּח קֹרַח דָּתָן וַאֲבִירָם״. But in my opinion there is no need, for it is fitting in the language to say ״וַיִּקַּח קֹרַח וְדָתָן וַיָּקוּמוּ וַיִּקָּהֲלוּ עַל מֹשֶׁה וְעַל אַהֲרֹן״, for at the beginning of every deed לְקִיחָה comes, and it is the language of setting in motion regarding that deed. And so ״וְאַבְשָׁלוֹם לָקַח וַיַּצֶּב לוֹ בְחַיָּיו אֶת מַצֶּבֶת״ (2 Samuel 18:18). And if you wish to interpret that the לְקִיחָה refers to the thing which he will mention afterward — Absalom took the pillar and set it up for himself in his lifetime — thus you may interpret: Korah took the men of the children of Israel, two hundred and fifty, and they rose up before Moses and assembled against Moses and against Aaron. And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Numbers 16:1) that this matter took place in the wilderness of Sinai when the firstborn were replaced and the Levites were set apart, for Israel thought that our master Moses did this of his own accord to grant greatness to his brother, also to the sons of Kohath who are close to him, and to all the sons of Levi who are of his family. And the Levites banded together against him because they were given over to Aaron and his sons, and the bond of Dathan and Abiram was because he removed the birthright from Reuben their father, and Korah too was a firstborn. And this is the opinion of Rabbi Abraham, who says in many places 'there is no earlier and later in the Torah' as he pleases, and I have already written (Numbers 9:1) that in my view the entire Torah is in sequence, except in a place where Scripture itself indicates the flashback or postponement, and even there for the sake of a matter and a correct reason. But this matter was in the wilderness of Paran, in Kadesh-barnea, after the affair of the spies. And the correct interpretation is that Korah grew angry over the chieftainship of Elzaphan, as our Rabbis said (Tanḥuma Korah 1), and he was also jealous of Aaron, as it says ״וּבִקַּשְׁתֶּם גַּם כְּהֻנָּה״ (Numbers 16:10), and Dathan and Abiram were drawn along with him — not over the birthright, for Jacob their father was the one who took it from Reuben and gave it to Joseph. But they too stated their complaint: ״לַהֲמִיתֵנוּ בַּמִּדְבָּר״ (Numbers 16:13) and ״לֹא אֶל אֶרֶץ זָבַת חָלָב וּדְבַשׁ הֲבִיאֹתָנוּ״ (Numbers 16:14). Now Israel, while they were in the wilderness of Sinai, suffered no evil whatsoever, for even in the matter of the calf, though the sin was great and widely known, the dead were few, and they were saved by the prayer of Moses, who prostrated himself on their behalf forty days and forty nights. They loved him as their own soul and obeyed him, and had any man rebelled against Moses at that time, the people would have stoned him. Therefore Korah bore the greatness of Aaron, and the firstborn bore the prerogative of the Levites and all the deeds of Moses. But when they came to the wilderness of Paran and were burned in the fire of Taberah and many died at Kivrot-ha-Ta'avah, and when they sinned with the spies Moses did not pray for them and the decree was not annulled, and the princes of all the tribes died in the plague before HaShem, and it was decreed upon all the people that they would perish in the wilderness and die there — then the spirit of all the people was bitter, and they said in their hearts that troubles would come upon them through the words of Moses. Then Korah found an opening to dispute his deeds, and he thought the people would listen to him. And this is the meaning of ״לַהֲמִיתֵנוּ בַּמִּדְבָּר״ — they said: behold, you have brought us to this place and you have not fulfilled what you vowed to give us, a land flowing with milk and honey, for you have given us no inheritance at all; rather we shall die in the wilderness and perish there, for our children too will never leave the wilderness, and what you vowed to them will be annulled as it was annulled for the fathers. And this is the meaning of their complaint here, in this place, immediately after the decree of the spies. And it is likely that all those who assembled were firstborn, for that is why the priesthood angered them, and therefore Moses told them to take firepans according to their former custom, and the matter would be revealed whether HaShem would choose them or the priests."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:06:40.269306502Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}